From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 06:48:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10278/11362] Close the two guard findings I left open: stale
 seals, and expiry demoting live sessions

Both were named in the adversarial review of REQ-INFRA-6820 and deliberately
deferred; neither should have stayed open overnight.

STALE SEALS FROZE THE REPOSITORY FOREVER. Staleness was applied to the
out-of-scope branch and not to the seal branch one block below it -- the seal
loop had zero staleness checks. A crashed session's declaration refused every
commit repo-wide the moment any sealed harness file legitimately changed, and
only deleting the scope file recovered it. That is the same "trusted and silent"
shape the expiry exists to prevent, in the branch the fix did not reach.

HEAD-ANCHORED SEALS ARE EXEMT BY DESIGN, and getting this wrong would have been
worse than the bug. `--seal-anchor head` marks a STANDING declaration -- the
conductor's, re-asserted at startup, which was 6.5h old against a 4h window when
this was written. Expiring it would have silently removed the only harness
protection in the repository: the exact failure this fix is for, delivered by
the fix for it. A HEAD-anchored seal is self-limiting anyway, since it compares
the working tree to HEAD and committing the change clears it. Only
declaration-anchored seals, which nothing clears, now expire.

EXPIRY WAS DEMOTING LIVE SESSIONS, NOT RELEASING ABANDONED ONES. `declared_at`
was rewritten on every declare, so a session working past the window lost its own
declared paths to any younger claimant -- and the refusal's own advice, to
re-declare, reset the clock, leaving it junior forever. Re-declaring the same run
id now keeps its original timestamp, so the record answers "when did this session
first claim this" rather than "when did it last type the command". A STALE prior
still starts a fresh clock, so reusing a dead id cannot resurrect expired
seniority.

Four mutations RED: seal staleness removed, head-anchored seals also expiring,
seniority reset on re-declare, and a stale prior resurrecting seniority. Restore
byte-identical. 99 tests green across the four affected files. Live check
verified both ways -- unidentified (how the conductor commits) exits 0, and the
standing declaration's seals still bite.

One test bug of my own, found and fixed while writing these: the first version
called `declare()` with an `--unseal` path that is not in SEALED_PATHS, so it
returned 1 without writing the scope file, and the test failed on a missing file
rather than on the behaviour it was measuring.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JntPrUGLNesPcJH1pyfFYw

The test-suite mutation gate armed a marker during this work naming exactly one
